INT. SUPREME COURT ANTEROOM - NIGHT

A tall mahogany room lined with law reports. A green banker's lamp. A black rotary telephone on a long cord.

ATTORNEY JULIAN ASHWORTH-BELLAMY, fifties, in shirtsleeves, paces with the receiver at his ear. His tie is loosened, his collar open.

JULIAN
Judge, with respect, the petition was filed before the deadline.

JUDGE PRENTISS (V.O.)
(over phone)
It was filed at 12:01, Mr. Ashworth-Bellamy.

JULIAN
By a clock that was running two minutes slow.

JUDGE PRENTISS (V.O.)
Then I'm afraid your clock is the court's problem no longer.

Julian stops pacing. He sets the lamp straight with one finger. Breathes.

JULIAN
Your Honor. A man's life is at stake.

JUDGE PRENTISS (V.O.)
And I've read every page of it.

JULIAN
Then you know.

JUDGE PRENTISS (V.O.)
I know the rules.

JULIAN
(rising)
To hell with the rules. He's going to die in six hours for a crime he did not commit!

A pause on the line. Julian slams his palm on the desk. The lamp rattles.

JUDGE PRENTISS (V.O.)
(quietly)
Then I suggest you spend the next six hours more wisely than shouting at me.

Click. Dial tone. Julian stands in the lamplight, the receiver still against his ear, as the hum fills the room.
